% --- Section I: Introduction ---
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

\IEEEPARstart{T}{he} exponential growth of video streaming services has fundamentally reshaped internet traffic patterns. With the advent of 4K/8K resolutions and the proliferation of mobile streaming over heterogeneous networks (e.g., 5G, LTE, WiFi), delivering high-quality video with minimal stalling (rebuffering) has become a paramount challenge. To address this, Adaptive Bitrate (ABR) streaming algorithms dynamically adjust the video bitrate for each chunk based on estimated network throughput and client buffer conditions.

The core objective of an ABR algorithm is to maximize the user's Quality of Experience (QoE), a metric that balances high visual quality against playback interruptions and smoothness. Traditional control-theoretic approaches, such as RobustMPC \cite{mpc} and Buffer-Based Approach (BBA) \cite{bba}, rely on fixed mathematical models to optimize bitrate decisions. While RobustMPC allows for explicit optimization over a prediction horizon, its performance is heavily dependent on the accuracy of throughput forecasts. As network conditions become increasingly stochastic, these prediction errors often lead to erratic behavior, manifesting as frequent quality switches (instability) that degrade the viewing experience.

In recent years, Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) has emerged as a promising alternative. The seminal work, Pensieve \cite{pensieve}, demonstrated that an ABR agent could learn to adapt to network variations purely through experience, outperforming fixed-rule algorithms. However, standard RL-based ABR agents suffer from a critical limitation: they are typically "blind" to the immediate characteristics of future video chunks. They treat the video as a sequence of generic blocks, unaware of whether the next chunk represents a complex action scene (requiring high bandwidth for acceptable quality) or a static scene. This lack of \textit{short-term foresight} makes standard RL agents reactive rather than proactive, often leading to suboptimal decisions—either becoming overly conservative to avoid rebuffering or failing to utilize available bandwidth efficiently.

In this paper, we propose a novel \textbf{Future-Aware PPO} agent that bridges the gap between the look-ahead planning of MPC and the adaptability of RL. We argue that exposing the agent to the \textit{sizes of the immediate next chunk} creates a powerful inductive bias, enabling the agent to anticipate buffer drains before they occur. Unlike prior works that use A3C, we employ Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO), which offers improved sample efficiency and training stability.

Our contributions are summarized as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Future-Aware State Space:} We design an augmented observation space that includes the precise byte-sizes of the next video chunk across all bitrate levels. This creates a "safety shield" effect, allowing the agent to identify risky high-bitrate decisions in volatile network conditions (e.g., \textit{CrowdRun} scenarios) without complex long-term forecasting.
    \item \textbf{Stability-Quality Balance:} Extensive evaluation on real-world network traces demonstrates that our method achieves a QoE of \textbf{3253}, outperforming the state-of-the-art Pensieve baseline (3185) by approximately \textbf{2.1\%}. More significantly, our approach reduces bitrate switching frequency by \textbf{$\sim$70\%} compared to RobustMPC (5.4 switches vs. 18.2 switches), solving the notorious instability problem of control-theoretic methods.
    \item \textbf{Robustness Analysis:} We provide a comprehensive ablation study and behavioral analysis, showing that our agent learns a sophisticated risk-averse policy—prioritizing stable mid-range bitrates during network volatility while aggressively capitalizing on bandwidth during stable periods.
\end{enumerate}

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows: Section \ref{sec:related} reviews related work. Section \ref{sec:system} details the system model and PPO formulation. Section \ref{sec:evaluation} presents the experimental results, and Section \ref{sec:conclusion} concludes the paper.
