\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

The evolution of ABR algorithms has transitioned from simple heuristics to sophisticated learning-based systems. We classify existing literature into three distinct paradigms: model-based control, reinforcement learning, and stability-oriented optimization.

\subsection{Model-Based and Predictive Control}
Early algorithms, such as BBA \cite{huang2014buffer}, relied on fixed heuristic rules based on buffer occupancy. While robust, they often fail to utilize available bandwidth efficiently during ramp-up phases. To address this, control-theoretic approaches like MPC \cite{yin2015control} and RobustMPC formulated bitrate selection as an optimization problem over a sliding horizon.
Recent advancements have focused on improving the throughput prediction component of MPC. For instance, \textit{Kairos} (2025) \cite{kairos2025} employs attention-based neural networks to forecast throughput with higher accuracy. However, these methods suffer from a fundamental limitation: they are computationally expensive and heavily dependent on the accuracy of the predictor. In highly stochastic environments (e.g., millimeter-wave 5G), even sophisticated predictors like Kairos can exhibit significant errors, leading to rebuffering. Our approach avoids this dependency by utilizing deterministic future chunk sizes ($\vec{v}_{next}$) from the manifest, eliminating prediction error for content size.

\subsection{Reinforcement Learning Approaches}
The application of Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) revolutionized ABR by enabling agents to learn policies directly from experience. Pensieve \cite{mao2017neural} set the benchmark by using A3C to outperform heuristics. Following this, \textit{Oboe} and \textit{Fugu} attempted to combine heuristics with learning.
More recently, \textit{Pensieve 5G} (2024) \cite{pensieve5g2024} adapted the state space for high-bandwidth volatile networks, and \textit{DeepReal} (2025) \cite{deepreal2025} proposed continuous control for short-form video streaming. Despite their success, these "black-box" RL methods lack mathematical stability guarantees. They prioritize average reward maximization, which often leads to aggressive behaviors and catastrophic stalls during rare "tail" events in network traces.

\subsection{Lyapunov-Based Stability}
Lyapunov optimization provides a theoretical framework for stabilizing queueing systems. Traditionally, it has been used in energy harvesting and generic packet scheduling. Recently, \textit{Fumos} (2024) \cite{fumos2024} successfully applied Lyapunov optimization to volumetric video streaming to manage massive bandwidth fluctuations.
However, pure Lyapunov methods often rely on simplified system models that do not capture the complex interplay of QoE metrics (e.g., visual smoothness vs. buffer risk). 

\subsection{Our Contribution: The Hybrid Approach}
Our work bridges the gap between the adaptability of RL and the rigorous stability of control theory. Unlike \textit{Kairos}, we do not rely on heavy throughput forecasting models. Unlike \textit{Pensieve 5G}, we do not blindly trust the neural network; instead, we inject a \textit{Lyapunov-guided drift penalty} into the reward function. This enforces a "soft constraint" that mathematically discourages the agent from entering dangerous buffer states, ensuring robustness without sacrificing the learning capability of PPO.

