\section{Performance Evaluation}
\label{sec:evaluation}
% --- Insert this AFTER \section{Experimental Evaluation} ---

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{subsec:setup}

\subsubsection{Network Traces and Video Dataset}
We evaluate our proposed method using a diverse set of network traces to ensure generalization across different environments:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{FCC Broadband Dataset:} A standard dataset comprising over 1 million throughput traces from broadband connections, representing stable but variable network conditions.
    \item \textbf{HSDPA (Norway) Dataset:} To test the agent's robustness in highly volatile scenarios, we utilized 3G/HSDPA mobile network traces collected from mobile scenarios (bus, train, ferry). These traces are characterized by sudden bandwidth drops and high variance.
\end{itemize}
For video content, we used the standard DASH reference dataset, including \textit{Big Buck Bunny}, \textit{Sintel}, and \textit{Tears of Steel}. Videos are encoded at multiple bitrate levels $\{300, 750, 1200, 1850, 2850, 4300\}$ kbps using the H.264/MPEG-4 codec. The chunk duration is fixed at 4 seconds.

\subsubsection{Implementation Details}
The proposed \textit{Lyapunov-Guided PPO} agent is implemented using PyTorch and the \textit{Stable-Baselines3} library. The actor and critic networks share a Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) feature extractor with two hidden layers of 64 units each and \textit{tanh} activation functions.
We trained the agent for 100,000 episodes using the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of $2.5 \times 10^{-4}$. The entropy coefficient was set to $0.01$ to encourage exploration during the early training phases. The clipping parameter $\epsilon$ for PPO was set to 0.2.
The Lyapunov penalty coefficient $\beta$ in the reward function was empirically tuned to $1.5$ to balance the trade-off between maximizing VMAF and minimizing buffer drift.

\subsubsection{Baselines}
We compare our approach against the following state-of-the-art algorithms:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Pensieve (RL-based) \cite{mao2017neural}:} The standard A3C-based agent. We use the pre-trained model provided by the authors as a reference for pure RL performance.
    \item \textbf{RobustMPC (Control-based) \cite{yin2015control}:} A model predictive control method that optimizes QoE over a sliding horizon, accounting for throughput prediction errors.
    \item \textbf{BBA (Heuristic) \cite{huang2014buffer}:} A buffer-based algorithm that linearly maps buffer occupancy to bitrate selection, serving as a baseline for stability.
\end{itemize}

